course: dictionary
lesson: 14904
title: Recur: happening again
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To recur means to happen again, often more than once. The start of the word, re, often means again, as in replay or redo. «The bell recurs every hour» means it rings again and again, every hour.
explain: Recur has a special meaning in maths. A recurring decimal has a digit or group of digits that repeats for ever. One third is 0.333…, so we write it as 0.3 recurring. The subject tells you which meaning to use: a maths book means decimals, and a doctor's note means an illness.
explain: The word can also be about thoughts. «The same dream recurred every night for a week» means the dream came back into her mind again and again. Ask yourself: is something coming back, or happening again? If it is, recur fits.
fact: Recur is a verb: recur, recurs, recurred, recurring.
fact: «Recur» means to happen again, often more than once.
fact: «Recurring decimal» means a decimal where a digit or group of digits repeats for ever, like 0.6 recurring.
fact: «Recurring» can describe an illness or problem that keeps coming back, like «a recurring headache».
fact: The re at the start of recur often means again.
exercise: «The bell recurs every hour, on the hour.» What does recurs mean here? | Stops for ever | Rings only once | Gets much louder | *Happens again and again
exercise: «He had a recurring headache all winter.» Which word tells you the headache kept coming back? | winter | *recurring | all | headache
exercise: Maths: 0.6 recurring means the digit 6 repeats for ever. What does a recurring decimal do? | Ends after one digit | Gets bigger every time | *Repeats a digit or group of digits for ever | Only has a decimal point
test: «The problem recurred after the new software was installed.» What does recurred mean? | Was fixed for ever | Was never noticed | Moved to a new place | *Happened again
test: Which number means 0.2 recurring? | 0.2 | *0.222… | 2.0 | 0.22
exam: «The same tune recurred in his head all afternoon.» Which meaning of recur fits? | To be sold out | *To come back into the mind again | To stop suddenly | To be measured
